The state unemployment insurance agency scenario is finished in `env/env_0049_state_unemployment_insurance_agency/`. The final whole-scenario check `validate_scenario` printed `VALID`: 5 difficulties, 10 tasks (all load and replay), 55 tools and 321 documents. The blueprint gates passed at the merge check, and I re-ran the document and traceability gates myself at the end; all were green.

**Difficulties** (2 tasks each). "Rounds" is how many audit rounds it took to pass, on the task/policy side and then on the published documents:

| Difficulty | Product | Rounds (policy / documents) |
|---|---|---|
| `weekly_certification_and_benefit_payment` | claim (79 docs) | 5 / 3 |
| `overpayment_waiver_and_recovery` | overpay (68 docs) | 3 / 1 |
| `benefit_and_tax_appeals` | appeal (47 docs) | 3 / 2 |
| `employer_quarterly_contributions` | taxacct (50 docs) | 4 / 1 |
| `employer_charge_protests` | charge (42 docs) | 6 / 2 |

Two products, identity verification (19 docs) and withholding (16 docs), belong to no difficulty. As the process intends, they passed their render gates but were never audited.

**What drove the extra rounds:**
- **Real policy defects.** Examples: an undeclared override of a base postponement rule, and factors labelled "method" that only swapped in a different number.
- **Rule text not matching the formula.** The document auditors found several places where a policy's wording and its calculation disagreed. I sent each back for a source fix even when no scored answer changed, then had it re-audited and the documents re-rendered.
- **Stale proof text.** Wrong sentences or arithmetic left in a task's rationale after an edit, with no effect on any score.
- **One render mistake.** The weekly-certification renderer cut part of a frozen blueprint description to make the documents read consistently; it was put back as written.

**Known issues left in place:** a few blueprint passages are stale or contradict the task-level overrides. For example, some parameter descriptions still describe rules that a difficulty's policy has replaced, and withholding's revocation rule contradicts itself. None of them changes any task's answer, so they are logged as known issues in `worklog.md` and appear in the published documents as written.

**Auditor flags that don't fail anything:** the auditors flagged several adjustment rules and rates as invented Lakeland-specific rules rather than standard practice. Examples are some answer-share haircuts and refund cushions. No audit failed on them, but they are the least realistic parts of the scenario. Also, after round 1 most re-audits were not fully blind, because the auditor had already seen the expected answers; each report says so.

**Run interruptions:** the run was cut off by account usage limits several times. Each time I checked what was actually on disk and resumed the same agent rather than starting over.

The complete start/end history is in `worklog.md`. I kept the masked views and the database query script the audit reports cite in `.audit_views/`; the loader ignores that folder. I also saved a memory note on how to brief the agents so these text-only rework rounds are avoided.
